<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>

    <!-- The colour of the strip Android exposes for a few frames when it resizes
         the WebView for the on-screen keyboard, before the keyboard is drawn.
         See AppTheme.NoActionBar in styles.xml and the screen-overlay filler in
         src/styles.css, which covers the other half of the same gap.

         ⚑ THIS DELIBERATELY DOES NOT MATCH THE SURFACE, BECAUSE IT CANNOT.
         The app sets its base colour per surface at runtime (surface.js: black
         lacquer 0a0908, dark wood 2c160d, rosewood 6d3210, slate 15161A), and a
         theme resource is fixed at build time. Making the window follow the
         surface would need a native call on every surface change — real work for
         a strip visible for about 60ms.

         So this is the near-black of Black lacquer, chosen because the thing
         ADJACENT to the strip during the flash is the keyboard, which is itself
         near-black — a far better compromise than the previous value (a light
         grey inherited from the platform default), which was wrong on every
         surface.

         ⚑ MEASURED 2026-08-05, and the original justification for this value
         did NOT survive the measurement. It used to claim the gap "reads as the
         keyboard's own shadow on every surface." Filmed on ROSEWOOD at 10x
         (`animator_duration_scale 10`), on a project drafting page: the region
         is flat #0A0908 across rows 1431-2195 of a 2340px screen — about a
         THIRD of the display, not a strip — and against rosewood's #6d3210 it
         reads as a dark band, not a shadow. Author's ruling: not a shadow, but
         good enough; the value stays.

         Two things follow for whoever reads this next. (1) Do not verify this
         on Black lacquer. Lacquer's desk IS #0A0908, so the native layer and
         the CSS filler are the same colour there and the check cannot fail —
         the same trap `checks/keyboard_gap.py` documents at its own line 58.
         Rosewood is the surface that discriminates. (2) The only thing that
         would remove this region entirely is stopping the WebView from being
         resized at all (Capacitor Keyboard `resize: 'none'` / Android
         `adjustNothing`), which hands every keyboard-avoidance behaviour to the
         app. Considered, not done. -->
    <color name="oela_window_gap">#0A0908</color>

</resources>
